WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.470
SPEAKER: When working
with the cloud,

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:03.137
many companies assume
you're trading off

00:00:03.137 --> 00:00:04.980
control for scalability.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.387
Thanks to Google Cloud's
new Bare Metal Solution,

00:00:07.387 --> 00:00:08.970
you can run your
specialized workloads

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:11.490
on Bare Metal servers
with full privileges

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:12.990
while having them
directly connected

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:14.665
to your cloud environment.

00:00:14.665 --> 00:00:16.290
This gives you the
best of both worlds,

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:18.660
letting you modernize your
applications while still

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.030
maintaining the investments
you've made in an existing

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:22.238
architecture.

00:00:22.238 --> 00:00:24.780
Let's take a look at migrating
an Oracle database to the Bare

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:26.940
Metal Solution and
adding more functionality

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:28.747
through Google Cloud Services.

00:00:28.747 --> 00:00:31.080
When you sign up for Google
Cloud's Bare Metal Solution,

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.600
you'll have two environments--
your Google Cloud environment

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.270
hosting your applications
and your Bare Metal Solution

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:37.622
in the region extension.

00:00:37.622 --> 00:00:40.080
These two are connected with
Cloud Interconnect, giving you

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.660
a direct and low latency
connection between them

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:46.050
and with all servers appearing
in the same network construct.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:47.760
Let's simplify and
break our application

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.733
into the front end and
the Oracle database.

00:00:50.733 --> 00:00:52.650
We can take the front
end and migrate directly

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:54.720
to Google Cloud using
Compute Engine to run

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.180
on virtual machines.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:57.870
We'll then migrate
our Oracle Database

00:00:57.870 --> 00:00:59.580
to the Bare Metal server.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:01.360
The architecture
would look like this

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.490
with different subnets
for each environment

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:06.140
but still able to communicate
over Interconnect.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:07.740
The VMs running
on Compute Engine

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:09.755
can directly interface
with your database

00:01:09.755 --> 00:01:11.880
while still being able to
access other Google Cloud

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.550
Services, like Cloud
Storage, letting you capture

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:16.650
the benefits of both worlds.

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.530
Now let's see it in action
with a sample migration.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:21.780
We'll start with a networking
page in the Google Cloud

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.730
console, where you can
see our current VPC

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:26.280
network for the Compute
Engine instances.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.170
Now we can head to the
Compute Engine page

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.290
to see our instances and connect
to one through the browser SSH

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:32.160
tool.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.150
Since these two
environments are connected,

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:36.120
we can jump to the Bare
Metal Solution server

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:37.990
from our virtual machine.

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:41.280
In this case, we'll run the SSH
command to directly hop over.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.050
In addition, we could
also connect over

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:45.537
any number of protocols
if we set them up,

00:01:45.537 --> 00:01:47.370
allowing just about any
specialized workload

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:48.570
to be migrated.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:50.325
Before we do the
example migration,

00:01:50.325 --> 00:01:52.950
let's make sure everything looks
good by running top and seeing

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.242
that we have full access to
all our Bare Metal machines

00:01:55.242 --> 00:01:56.118
resources.

00:01:56.118 --> 00:01:57.660
This is a physical
machine, and we're

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:00.060
running Red Hat Enterprise,
so you can manage software

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:02.190
the same way you do with
your on-prem servers,

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:04.420
like with the Yum
package manager.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:06.030
We also have the
benefit of the cloud,

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:07.920
so we'll use the Google
Cloud command line

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.930
interface right on the machine.

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:12.770
We can run gsutil to
download an Oracle RMAN

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:16.140
backup uploaded from
on-premise onto the machine.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.300
After that, we can run
the short script, which

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.850
creates a database,
then imports the data.

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:22.860
If you have a gold
image of your database,

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.290
it's now possible to spin
up multiple dev environments

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:28.080
and have them all connected
to your cloud resources.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.810
This solution also works for
real application clusters,

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:33.493
letting you manage high
availability setups.

00:02:33.493 --> 00:02:35.160
Now that the database
has been restored,

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.540
let's take a look at the data.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.977
Since both environments have
direct access to each other,

00:02:38.977 --> 00:02:41.310
you can run the same tools
you're already familiar with,

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:42.863
such a SQL Developer.

00:02:42.863 --> 00:02:45.030
We can see here that the
database has been restored,

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:47.308
and our application
can connect directly.

00:02:47.308 --> 00:02:49.350
Once again, the Interconnect
lets your cloud apps

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:52.333
communicate with the Bare Metal
server in any number of ways.

00:02:52.333 --> 00:02:54.000
Now that we've seen
how you can migrate,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.167
let's try to enrich the
data with other Google Cloud

00:02:56.167 --> 00:02:56.970
Services.

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:59.610
We'll start by exporting the
data from our running database.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:01.900
Once again, we can use gsutil.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:03.450
But this time, we'll
upload the file

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:06.197
from our Bare Metal machine
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:03:06.197 --> 00:03:08.280
You could also set up
streaming over Interconnect,

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:11.025
but we'll keep it simple for
now by just using a file.

00:03:11.025 --> 00:03:12.900
Let's switch back to
the Google Cloud console

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:14.160
and head to BigQuery.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.710
With the Oracle Database export
uploaded to Cloud Storage,

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:18.630
it's easy to query the
data from BigQuery,

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:21.073
giving you the ability to
run large analysis jobs.

00:03:21.073 --> 00:03:22.740
You can see a preview
of the data that's

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.350
been exported from the
database, and you can even share

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:26.950
the table with other users.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:29.820
BigQuery also connects to many
other tools like Data Studio,

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:32.037
giving you an easy way
to visualize the data.

00:03:32.037 --> 00:03:34.620
We can switch to Data Studio and
quickly build a visual report

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:35.715
of our application data.

00:03:35.715 --> 00:03:37.590
If we had set up streaming
between the Oracle

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:39.510
Database and BigQuery,
these reports

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:42.750
would be automatically updated
with new data as it comes in.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:45.060
In addition, you can also
load your cloud storage data

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.010
into other tools
like AI Platform

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:48.633
to build custom ML models.

00:03:48.633 --> 00:03:50.550
By connecting the cloud
to Bare Metal servers,

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:52.383
you can modernize your
specialized workloads

00:03:52.383 --> 00:03:54.870
with minimal disruption on
top of being able to innovate

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:56.550
with Cloud Services.

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:58.830
If you're interested in
learning more, visit this URL

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:00.420
and contact your sales team.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:03.770
[MUSIC PLAYING]

